#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176659} :activity/id #uuid "e5996434-c24a-5444-b4f1-fc98c25c7861" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048273 :topic/id #uuid "8e9bd4e1-2ee1-5d4f-a80d-e50ca56a2f32" :topic/math-academy-id 3584} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176957 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the polar curve\n\n$$\nr=1+\\cos\\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below:\n\n![](images/a2/a23339bd8b2777355a7b146319958739cbed296f9a36ae5cc19d18c2890d4316.png)\n\nThis polar curve has **horizontal tangents** at the points $A$ and $B,$ shown above. Our goal is to compute the value of $\\theta$ at each point.\n\nAt the horizontal tangents, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the slope of the tangent line to a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, at the points $A$ and $B,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = 0.\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be satisfied for some value of $\\theta,$ we require that the *numerator* is zero while the *denominator* is nonzero. Therefore, we must find the points where\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad x'(\\theta) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIt's essential to check that $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are zero and nonzero, respectively. If they are *both* zero, then this leads to \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Points where $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are both zero require further investigation. \n\nLet's now compute the values of $\\theta$ at the points $A$ and $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0f960cf2-5a42-534c-b80f-277d68f5839e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1122 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b5d8565-6824-5993-920e-20d470c0d57b" :step/math-academy-id 644655 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175793 :question/id #uuid "ba03d3ad-adb7-5c30-9d44-181a1980d125" :question/math-academy-id "e-630"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca410330-0448-5503-b5f6-37021667a130" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549043}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where the Tangent Is Horizontal"} :step/id #uuid "2947f175-d07a-520e-8c43-6643dae9f99e" :step/math-academy-id 644657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177054 :tutorial/content "![](images/7a/7a1647aa543ef6b9c4c2004e358a91b8d034bd8d72cc53e72bf0dc69266366bc.png)\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $y'(\\theta)=0.$\n\nWriting down the equation $y'(\\theta) = 0$ and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0, \\qquad \\cos\\theta + 1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta + 1=0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have three candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $x'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into the expression for $x'(\\theta).$ If $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.  \n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+1) \\\\  & =-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_2 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have horizontal tangents where $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ These correspond to the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively.\n\n- This analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons.\n\nFor the remainder of this lesson, we will not concern ourselves with the case where $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are both zero. However, it's worth carrying out step 3 regardless when solving these problems, as this will help develop good habits that will come in handy later." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2158ee2-ecde-50ce-b68e-b23dde7e3b42" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6268 :tutorial/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "001f9237-70db-50c2-b022-9357d821e175" :step/math-academy-id 644656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176043 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175691 :question/id #uuid "f67abf5c-1352-5fd0-8365-0164c47a28a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10245"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5517548-8939-5bf5-a769-9f99a2aa1029" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549064}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where the Tangent Is Vertical"} :step/id #uuid "82924288-561b-592d-afe5-787d4d17d21c" :step/math-academy-id 644662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177060 :tutorial/content "![](images/20/20b8d6a35a9925c0442830f0abd20c522f0e3a420d27e6239e43120edbfc55d3.png)\n\nLet's revisit the following polar curve:\n\n$$\nr=1+\\cos\\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThis polar curve has **vertical tangents** at the points $P, Q,$ and $R,$ shown above. We wish to compute the values of $\\theta$ at these points.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative becomes infinitely large as we approach the points $P, Q,$ and $R,$ which means that the *denominator* must approach zero while the *numerator* remains finite.  \n\nTherefore, to find the values of $\\theta$ at these points, we must find the points where\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad y'(\\theta) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIt's important to check that $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$ are zero and nonzero, respectively. If they are *both* zero, then this leads to \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Points where $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are both zero require further investigation. \n\nLet's now compute the values of $\\theta$ at these points." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5c962fc-ed5e-5c73-9a8a-3db574c69cf4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6385 :tutorial/title "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "1b164092-19c3-5c8e-b08e-624daf8d58c0" :step/math-academy-id 644660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177061 :tutorial/content "![](images/20/20b8d6a35a9925c0442830f0abd20c522f0e3a420d27e6239e43120edbfc55d3.png)\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $x'(\\theta)=0{:}$\n\nWriting down the equation $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0, \\qquad 2\\cos\\theta+1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = 0,\\pi.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta+1= 0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have four candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 =0, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $y'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $y'(\\theta)$ and check that $y'(\\theta) \\neq 0.$ If $y'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-1 \\\\  & =2+1-1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_3 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =2-1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_3 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have vertical tangents where $\\theta = 0, \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$ These correspond to the points $P, Q,$ and $R,$ respectively.\n\n- Again, this analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "afebb67f-3a1e-5c0a-95b1-9a5065f21f09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6386 :tutorial/title "Finding Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "04b36ea9-c35a-5ca1-8664-88bd298e1711" :step/math-academy-id 644661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176848}}] :activity/title "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176130}]}
